---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Sync" description="Real-time sync in SwirlDB - how clients stay in sync">
  <h1>Sync</h1>

  <p>
    SwirlDB syncs via WebSocket using a binary protocol. Built on Automerge CRDTs,
    changes merge automatically with no conflicts. Clients work offline and catch up when reconnected.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>How It Works</h2>
    <div class="card highlight">
      <pre><code>{`┌─────────┐                           ┌─────────┐
│ Browser │  ─── Push Changes ────→  │  Rust   │
│  WASM   │  ←── Broadcast ────────  │ Server  │
└─────────┘                           └─────────┘
                                           ↑
┌─────────┐                                │
│ Browser │  ─── Push Changes ─────────────┘
│  WASM   │  ←── Broadcast ─────────────────
└─────────┘

1. Client connects with current "heads" (version markers)
2. Server sends back any changes the client is missing
3. Client pushes local changes → Server broadcasts to others
4. All changes merge conflict-free via Automerge CRDT`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Connecting</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Browser (WASM-managed WebSocket)</h3>
      <p>The WASM layer handles the entire protocol internally - encoding, decoding, head tracking, and change merging.</p>
      <pre><code>{`import { SwirlDB } from '@swirldb/js';

const db = await SwirlDB.withIndexedDB('my-app');

// Connect to server with client ID and subscriptions
db.connect('ws://localhost:3030/ws', 'my-client-id', ['/**']);

// Write data - changes sync automatically
db.data.user.name = 'Alice';
db.data.messages = [{ from: 'Alice', text: 'Hello!' }];

// Observe remote changes
db.data.messages.$observe(() => {
  console.log('Messages updated:', db.data.messages.$value);
});

// Push changes manually (or let WASM handle it)
db.syncChanges();`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Subscription Filtering</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <p>Clients subscribe to path patterns. The server only broadcasts changes that match a client's subscriptions.</p>
      <pre><code>{`// Subscribe to everything
db.connect(url, clientId, ['/**']);

// Subscribe to specific paths only
db.connect(url, clientId, ['/messages/**', '/user/alice/**']);

// What happens:
// - Another client writes to /settings/theme = "dark"
// - Server checks: does /settings/theme match /messages/** or /user/alice/**?
// - No match → this client does NOT receive the broadcast
// - Reduces bandwidth and keeps clients focused`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Binary Protocol</h2>
    <div class="card highlight">
      <p>All sync uses a compact binary protocol over WebSocket (~10x smaller than JSON):</p>
      <pre><code>{`Message Types:
┌───────────────┬──────┬────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Type          │ Code │ Description                            │
├───────────────┼──────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ MSG_CONNECT   │ 0x01 │ Client connects with heads & subs     │
│ MSG_SYNC      │ 0x02 │ Server sends missing changes          │
│ MSG_PUSH      │ 0x03 │ Client pushes local changes           │
│ MSG_BROADCAST │ 0x04 │ Server broadcasts to subscribers      │
│ MSG_PUSH_ACK  │ 0x05 │ Server acknowledges push              │
│ MSG_SUBSCRIBE │ 0x06 │ Client updates subscriptions          │
│ MSG_SUB_ACK   │ 0x07 │ Server confirms subscription changes  │
└───────────────┴──────┴────────────────────────────────────────┘

Frame: [1 byte type][variable payload]

Payload contains binary Automerge changes + heads (32-byte hashes)`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Connection Flow</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <pre><code>{`1. CLIENT → SERVER: MSG_CONNECT
   - Client ID: "alice"
   - Subscriptions: ["/**"]
   - Heads: [hash1, hash2]  ← "I have everything up to these versions"

2. SERVER → CLIENT: MSG_SUBSCRIBE_ACK
   - Added: ["/**"]
   - Denied: []  ← Policy engine may deny some subscriptions

3. SERVER → CLIENT: MSG_SYNC
   - Server heads: [hash3, hash4]
   - Changes: [change1, change2, ...]  ← Only what client is missing

4. CLIENT → SERVER: MSG_PUSH (when client writes)
   - Client heads: [newHash]
   - Changes: [newChange]

5. SERVER → CLIENT: MSG_PUSH_ACK
   - Server heads after merge

6. SERVER → OTHER CLIENTS: MSG_BROADCAST
   - Only sent to clients whose subscriptions match the affected paths`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Incremental Sync</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <p>
        Clients track "heads" - cryptographic hashes of the latest CRDT changes they've seen (like git commits).
        On connect, the client sends its heads. The server computes the delta and sends only missing changes.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`// First connect: client has no heads
// → Server sends ALL changes (full state)

// Reconnect after disconnect: client sends current heads
// → Server sends only changes since those heads

// Result: Minimal bandwidth, fast reconnection`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Conflict Resolution</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <p>Automerge CRDTs merge changes automatically. No manual conflict resolution needed.</p>
      <pre><code>{`Scenario                  │ Resolution
──────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────
Different fields          │ Both changes kept
Same field, different val │ Last-write-wins (by timestamp)
List inserts              │ Position-based merge
Nested objects            │ Per-key merge
Deletes                   │ Delete wins over concurrent edits

Example:
  Alice (offline): db.data.user.name = "Alice"
  Bob (offline):   db.data.user.age = 30

  After sync: { user: { name: "Alice", age: 30 } }
  → No conflict, both changes merged`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Offline Support</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <p>Changes work locally whether connected or not. The WASM layer queues changes and syncs when reconnected.</p>
      <pre><code>{`// Works offline - changes saved to IndexedDB
db.data.drafts.post1 = { title: 'New Post', content: '...' };
db.persist();

// When connection is restored:
// 1. WASM reconnects with exponential backoff
// 2. Sends current heads to server
// 3. Receives any missed changes
// 4. Pushes queued local changes
// 5. All clients converge to same state`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Server Deployment</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Run the Server</h3>
      <pre><code>{`# Build
cd native/swirldb-server
cargo build --release

# Run
RUST_LOG=info ./target/release/swirldb-server

# Or with config
RUST_LOG=info ./target/release/swirldb-server --config swirldb.config.json`}</code></pre>

      <p>The server is a single static binary. No runtime dependencies. Listens on port 3030 by default.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Docker</h3>
      <pre><code>{`FROM rust:1.70 as builder
WORKDIR /app
COPY . .
RUN cargo build --release --bin swirldb-server

FROM debian:bookworm-slim
COPY --from=builder /app/target/release/swirldb-server /usr/local/bin/
EXPOSE 3030
VOLUME /data
CMD ["swirldb-server"]`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Security</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Policy-Based Access Control</h3>
      <p>The server validates subscriptions against the policy engine. Clients can only subscribe to paths they're authorized for.</p>
      <pre><code>{`// Server config (swirldb.config.json)
{
  "policies": {
    "rules": [
      {
        "path": "/user/{actor.id}/**",
        "actors": [{ "match": "id", "value": "{actor.id}" }],
        "actions": ["read", "write", "subscribe"],
        "effect": "allow",
        "priority": 10
      },
      {
        "path": "/public/**",
        "actors": [{ "match": "any" }],
        "actions": ["read", "subscribe"],
        "effect": "allow",
        "priority": 20
      }
    ]
  }
}

// Client subscribes to ["/user/alice/**", "/admin/**"]
// Server responds:
//   Added: ["/user/alice/**"]   ← allowed by policy
//   Denied: ["/admin/**"]       ← denied by policy`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Performance</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Binary protocol:</strong> ~10x smaller than JSON</li>
        <li><strong>Delta sync:</strong> Only transmit changes since last sync</li>
        <li><strong>Subscription filtering:</strong> Clients only receive relevant broadcasts</li>
        <li><strong>Batching:</strong> Multiple rapid changes sent as single message</li>
        <li><strong>Reconnection:</strong> Exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, max 60s)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .card {
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid #e1e1e1;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  }

  .card.highlight {
    border-left: 4px solid #667eea;
    background: #f9f9ff;
    padding: 2rem;
  }

  ul {
    margin-left: 2rem;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  li {
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }

  section {
    margin: 3rem 0;
  }
</style>
